Context: this is a 1986 draft, 'Vers une géométrie des formes (topologiques)'. It builds combinatorial models of 1-dimensional 'forms' (curves). A model is X = (L, S, ∂), where:
- L is a set of points;
- S ⊂ P(L) is a family of 'segments' (intervals);
- ∂ : S → P_2(L) sends each segment to its two-element set of endpoints.
For each point x there is a two-element set B_x of 'bandes', i.e. local sides or half-branches at x. A segment I defines a band β(I,x) at each of its endpoints. The previous page defined orientation of a segment (a choice of endpoint, equivalently of one of its two canonical orders). It also defined π_0(X) through the equivalence relation generated by 'x and y lie in a common closed segment'.

This page does two things.
(1) Reduction to the connected case. A model decomposes into its connected components. If X is regular (L = L_r, condition H1), each component is regular. So studying regular models reduces to studying connected regular models.
(2) Orientation theorem for a connected regular model. One can find, essentially uniquely, three things:
- a transitive system of bijections among the two-element sets ∂I (I ∈ S), giving a colimit 2-set ω_S;
- a transitive system of bijections among the B_x (x ∈ L), giving ω_L;
- a bijection ω_S ≅ ω_L. Call the common 2-set ω; it comes with isomorphisms ω ≅ ∂I and ω ≅ B_x.
The compatibility conditions are spelled out on the next page:
- (a) if J ⊂ I, the bijection ∂I ≅ ∂J sends the smallest element of I to the smallest element of J, for either canonical order;
- (b) the initial endpoint x_0 of I corresponds to the band at x_0 defined by I.
Equivalently: the equivalence relation on ∐_I ∂I ⊔ ∐_x B_x generated by (a) and (b) has exactly two classes.

In modern terms, ω is the set of orientations of a connected 1-dimensional space. The statement is that the orientation double cover, built combinatorially from segments and local sides, is trivial with exactly two global sections. So a connected 1-manifold-like model has exactly two orientations, each determined by orienting any single segment or any single point. The margin sketch (an arc with arrows and a thick segment) illustrates propagating an orientation along a curve.
